00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:09.360
So, Jonty, can you take
us-- how are we doing?

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:11.850
Can you take us onto how
machine learning is directly

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:15.390
providing your customers
with a better buying

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:16.830
experience and service?

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:19.440
JONTY ANGEL: Yes.

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:26.020
So just first, to introduce what
is a benefit for our customers,

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:29.810
I would like to share the
three core areas that we look

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:33.930
every day to improve
on is, number one,

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:36.600
we need to make sure that
deliveries are on time.

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:39.180
It's more important to
get a delivery on time

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:41.790
than anything, because
that's the expectation

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:43.600
of the customer.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:45.850
The second is great shelf life.

00:25:45.850 --> 00:25:49.050
So we need to make sure that all
our products have a great shelf

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:53.580
life so that they are in the
life of before they expire.

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:58.440
And actually, Ocado has
a life promise in that.

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:00.540
And then almost
no substitutions.

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.680
So that means when
you place an order,

00:26:02.680 --> 00:26:04.800
you want those products
that you ordered.

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:07.500
You don't want to see a
whole list of other products

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:10.260
which we just threw in there.

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:11.940
So you want the
products you ordered.

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:14.280
So our supply chain
systems need to be

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:17.310
efficient and accurate to
ensure that the right stock is

00:26:17.310 --> 00:26:18.420
in the right warehouse.

00:26:21.030 --> 00:26:25.560
If I go on to the benefits
to retailers, well,

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:28.620
if we aren't focusing on
building the technology,

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:31.860
then that takes a lot
of the risk off them,

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:34.890
and they can effectively focus
on running their business.

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:37.860
So that gives us an
opportunity to develop

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:43.150
more intelligent systems for
the retailer to benefit from.

00:26:43.150 --> 00:26:46.980
So for example, one of
the benefits is low waste.

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:50.430
And that's because, in
our customer journey,

00:26:50.430 --> 00:26:53.490
we have different mechanisms
of offering products

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:55.590
throughout the
journey that ensure

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:58.980
that maybe those products
that were going to be wasted

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:02.760
can be re-offered within
the customer journey.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:05.160
There's another one called
know your customer--

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:06.930
which I'll talk about
a little bit later--

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:09.690
which is around
customization, where we do

00:27:09.690 --> 00:27:12.300
use machine learning to
get a better understanding

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:16.560
of the needs of our customer
based on previous orders

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:20.320
so that we can deliver a
more personalized experience.

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:24.600
And then, there's a couple
of other ones over here

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:27.891
which add to the overall
benefit that a retailer gets

00:27:27.891 --> 00:27:28.890
from using our platform.

00:27:31.750 --> 00:27:36.100
So more specifically,
to answer your question,

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:39.610
the two areas that
I'll briefly highlight

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:43.540
where we've used machine
learning to positively impact

00:27:43.540 --> 00:27:46.690
our customer experience
and our platform

00:27:46.690 --> 00:27:51.490
has been in fraud detection
and in customization.

00:27:51.490 --> 00:27:55.840
So I have a little
quote up here which

00:27:55.840 --> 00:28:01.810
shares the motivation of why we
wanted to use machine learning

00:28:01.810 --> 00:28:03.280
for fraud detection.

00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:05.650
And it was really
all about, how can

00:28:05.650 --> 00:28:08.830
we have speed and adaptability?

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:12.850
So we know that
if we were humans

00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:15.580
looking at fraudulent
activity and trying

00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:18.730
to make assessments and
predictions, what would happen

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:22.030
is that the fraudsters
would effectively

00:28:22.030 --> 00:28:25.240
change the way that
they operate in a way

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:28.330
where we would need to be
really, really quick to keep up

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:29.390
with them.

00:28:29.390 --> 00:28:31.390
So what we felt is that
this is a perfect use

00:28:31.390 --> 00:28:34.330
case for machine learning,
where we could just

00:28:34.330 --> 00:28:35.780
let a machine do that.

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:39.632
And if we have enough
data in the background

00:28:39.632 --> 00:28:43.180
to power that
engine, the machines

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:45.820
would become more intelligent
and would understand how

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:48.280
to spot fraudulent behavior.

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:52.915
Actually, my colleagues
spoke a bit more about this

00:28:52.915 --> 00:28:54.250
in another session.

00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:56.800
I think it was yesterday.

00:28:56.800 --> 00:29:00.250
And there's a video which
explains more technically

00:29:00.250 --> 00:29:01.810
what we're doing there.

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:08.260
But at a high level, the
data sets that we're using

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:12.400
are things like past orders,
previous cases of fraud,

00:29:12.400 --> 00:29:16.210
past deliveries, the cost
of baskets, order items.

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:19.120
So you know that if
someone is effectively,

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:24.880
every morning at 9:00
AM, buying X amounts of--

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:29.860
I don't know-- alcohol
and they are just

00:29:29.860 --> 00:29:34.840
being placed in these
random delivery spots,

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:38.620
you can assume it's
fraudulent activity.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:41.770
But the powerful thing is taking
all these data sets together,

00:29:41.770 --> 00:29:44.890
you get new trends
and you get new ideas.

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:46.960
And you can actually,
for the first time,

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:50.740
stay ahead of the
fraudulent activity.

00:29:50.740 --> 00:29:54.760
With fraud, what's interesting
is, in our customer journey,

00:29:54.760 --> 00:29:58.510
actually, we do
our fraud detection

00:29:58.510 --> 00:30:01.360
at the front end of
that customer journey.

00:30:01.360 --> 00:30:06.490
So effectively, when you enter
the site to start shopping,

00:30:06.490 --> 00:30:09.835
we are already doing
the fraud checks

00:30:09.835 --> 00:30:12.970
and starting to assess
fraudulent activity.

00:30:12.970 --> 00:30:15.130
And that's because
we want to make

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:21.130
sure that anything that happens
after that is not fraudulent.

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:23.650
Some of the tools
that we use are we

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:28.540
really try use the whole
stack of Google Cloud.

00:30:28.540 --> 00:30:32.440
So we use the ML Engine,
BigQuery, Cloud Storage,

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:33.970
and TensorFlow.

00:30:33.970 --> 00:30:36.850
And this is a really,
really big project

00:30:36.850 --> 00:30:38.850
right now within our business.

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:44.570
This is another
diagram which just

00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:46.910
gives that whole data flow.

00:30:52.170 --> 00:30:57.330
So the last section
is customization.

00:30:57.330 --> 00:31:02.730
So if, again, you look at the
end-to-end customer journey

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:08.400
where you start an order,
you start doing your shopping

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:10.230
and preparing your
order, and then you

00:31:10.230 --> 00:31:12.600
go through to
completing your order,

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:17.010
to checkout, what we
found is that the real way

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:21.300
to impact a customer
is to show them

00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:24.540
things at the end of
their customer journey

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:28.530
which will allow them to
have an amazing experience.

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:31.590
And you can now do that
using machine learning.

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:34.080
Because the way
we've employed it

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:36.870
is we get to know more
about our customer

00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:39.990
based on their previous
orders and favorites

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:44.330
so that we can offer them things
in the last pages of checkout

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:46.830
which they wouldn't necessarily
think they could be offered.

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:50.130
For example, the page
that we offer is called,

00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:51.810
have you run out of?

00:31:51.810 --> 00:31:57.480
And that is a system where,
if I always, every third week,

00:31:57.480 --> 00:32:01.950
buy dishwashing tablets,
and I get to that third week

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:04.890
where I've now
completed my basket

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:07.650
and the system has
recognized that I don't have

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:10.050
the dishwasher tablets,
it will prompt me

00:32:10.050 --> 00:32:12.780
and it will say, well,
have you run out of this?

00:32:12.780 --> 00:32:16.260
Do you expect to buy these?

00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:18.120
That's an amazing service.

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:21.660
Because if I can add
that to my basket

00:32:21.660 --> 00:32:24.840
without having to
see it not being

00:32:24.840 --> 00:32:29.100
in my order and the frustration
that comes after that,

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:31.590
I'd be an extremely
happy customer.

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:33.150
So the benefit there
to the customer

00:32:33.150 --> 00:32:39.450
is that they get to not be
inconvenienced by products

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:41.520
that they leave
out of their shop

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:43.200
because they've forgotten it.

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:45.720
So they're doing their
shop for a purpose.

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:48.420
And sometimes it's those
products that they leave out.

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:51.600
For the retailer, this
is massively beneficial

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:55.230
because the retailer gets
to build a bigger basket.

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:59.610
So it gets to make sure that
the customer is both happy

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:03.060
and the basket is
increasing in size

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:05.880
with those essential items.

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:11.370
And there, again, we use a
large extent of the Google Cloud

00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:17.490
to build this machine
learning engine.

00:33:17.490 --> 00:33:22.700
So that's really what I
wanted to cover today.

00:33:22.700 --> 00:33:25.127
And I hope I've
answered your questions.

00:33:25.127 --> 00:33:26.210
NICK MARTIN: I believe so.

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:27.100
Thank you, Jonty.

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:28.820
That's been-- well,
I'm sure you'll

00:33:28.820 --> 00:33:31.640
agree with me-- a fantastic
insight into the Ocado business

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:34.920
model and also use of
technology and machine learning.

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:36.964
So a huge thank you.

00:33:36.964 --> 00:33:37.880
JONTY ANGEL: Pleasure.

00:33:37.880 --> 00:33:39.921
NICK MARTIN: Thank you,
everybody, for attending.

